    <fr:title text="Subobject Classifier">Subobject Classifier</fr:title>
    <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
  </fr:frontmatter>
  <fr:mainmatter>
    <fr:p>Subsets <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex> of a set <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[X]]></fr:tex> may classically be identified with a characteristic map <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\chi _{A} : X \to  \mathsf {bool}]]></fr:tex>. Intuitively, for every <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[x : X]]></fr:tex>, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\chi _{A}]]></fr:tex> gives a truth value to the statement "<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[x]]></fr:tex> is in the subset <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex>". In this manner, the domain of the characteristic map, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathsf {bool}]]></fr:tex>, classifies the subsets of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[X]]></fr:tex>.</fr:p>
    <fr:p>Generalizing over this principle, in a category <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathcal {C}]]></fr:tex> an object <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\Omega ]]></fr:tex> is a subobject classifier if maps into it from some object <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[c]]></fr:tex> likewise uniquely identify a subobject of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[c]]></fr:tex>.</fr:p>
    <fr:p>We can understand <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\Omega ]]></fr:tex> to behave like an object of truth values that are not necessarily boolean valued. A morphism <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[p : c \to  \Omega ]]></fr:tex> can be thought of like a predicate on <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[c]]></fr:tex>. If <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[c]]></fr:tex> were a set, this would precisely be the characteristic function on it. However, this idea can generalize beyond sets. For instance in the category of graphs, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\Omega ]]></fr:tex> is a cleverly constructed graph such that any graph homomorphism <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[g \to  \Omega ]]></fr:tex> into it picks out a unique subgraph of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[g]]></fr:tex>.</fr:p>
    <fr:p>There are always two (suggestively named) disjoint "points" of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\Omega ]]></fr:tex>, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathsf {true} , \mathsf {false} : \top  \to  \Omega ]]></fr:tex>. I think if we're being careful, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathsf {true}]]></fr:tex> may properly be the "subobject classifier" but I always use the term to refer to <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\Omega ]]></fr:tex> itself.</fr:p>
  </fr:mainmatter>
